  This project is NOT affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by 
  The XFree86 Project, Inc.

  The original project has been dormant since 2011. This repository contains 
  patches required to compile and run the XFree86 4.3.0/4.8.1 codebase on 
  modern operating systems (tested on Arch Linux).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  W H A T   I S   T H I S ?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This is a resurrection of the classic XFree86 codebase. 
  
  Do NOT expect this to run on your bare metal GPU with 3D acceleration. 
  The primary functional target of this release is the VIRTUAL FRAMEBUFFER 
  SERVER (Xvfb) running in 24-bit TrueColor.

  Why? Because porting the entire XFree86 driver architecture to modern 
  kernels is a nightmare. The Xvfb allows us to preserve the legacy X11 
  rendering (and fix old bugs) without touching modern DRM/KMS subsystems.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  W H A T   W O R K S ?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [x] Xvfb (Virtual Framebuffer) - 24-bit TrueColor rendering.
  [x] Legacy xsetroot, xeyes, xterm.
  [x] RGB Database generation (rgb.db, rgb.dir).
  [x] Fixed the infamous 2003 "EvenStipple" rendering bug (fbgc.c).
  
  W H A T   D O E S   N O T   W O R K ?
  [ ] XFree86 hardware drivers (VESA/FBDEV).
  [ ] Modern XInput2 (XInputExtension is missing).
  [ ] XRender/Composite (use flat colors, kids).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  B U I L D I N G   ( O N   A R C H   L I N U X )
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WARNING: The Imake build system is older than you. It WILL complain. 
  Read the instructions carefully. Do not run as root.

  1. Install build dependencies:
     $ sudo pacman -S base-devel libgdbm-compat xbitmaps

  2. Fix the hardcoded paths (The "Not-In-/usr" fix):
     Edit `xc/programs/Xserver/include/site.h` around line 81.
     Change: #define RGB_DB "/usr/lib/X11/rgb"
     To:     #define RGB_DB "./rgb"

  3. Build the core server:
     $ cd xc
     $ make World
     (If you know a faster way with Imake, let us know. Otherwise, go get a coffee.)

  4. Build the RGB database (The manual way):
     $ cd programs/rgb
     $ gcc -o rgb rgb.c -I../Xserver/include -lgdbm_compat -lgdbm
     $ ./rgb < rgb.txt
     (This generates rgb.dir and rgb.pag. Don't ask why it needs gdbm, it's 2003.)

  5. Build the legacy xsetroot:
     $ cd programs/xsetroot
     $ gcc -o xsetroot xsetroot.c -I../../include -lX11 -lXmu

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  R U N N I N G
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  To start the server on display :10 with 24-bit TrueColor and the correct 
  font and color paths:

  $ ./xc/programs/Xserver/Xvfb :10 -screen 0 800x600x24+32 -cc 4 \
      -co /absolute/path/to/xc/programs/rgb/rgb \
      -fp /absolute/path/to/your/fonts/misc \
      -ac -nolisten tcp &

  Test it:
  $ ./xc/programs/xsetroot/xsetroot -display :10 -solid '#4682b4'
  $ xterm -display :10 &

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  K N O W N   I S S U E S
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - The server will scream about "SecurityPolicy" file missing. Ignore it.
  - The server might look for fonts in weird places. Use the -fp flag.
  - If the screen is a weird checkerboard pattern, you forgot the -cc 4 flag 
    or your RGB database is empty.

